WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.254
Module six:

2
00:00:01.254 --> 00:00:02.240
asset types.

3
00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:08.238
Coins, tokens and memecoins, options, dated futures and tokenized assets,

4
00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:10.046
and the risks of each.

5
00:00:11.046 --> 00:00:13.511
Network coins pay for the network's work,

6
00:00:13.511 --> 00:00:18.021
protocol tokens give rights in an application, stablecoins hold the dollar,

7
00:00:18.021 --> 00:00:20.486
and memecoins give nothing but attention.

8
00:00:20.486 --> 00:00:22.650
With any coin the tokenomics matter:

9
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.137
how many there are in total, how many are already trading,

10
00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:28.302
and what will reach the market next.

11
00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:32.876
Market cap is the price times the coins in circulation.

12
00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:37.881
Fully diluted value is the price times all the coins that will ever come out.

13
00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:41.975
If 15 percent are circulating, the other 85 belong to the team,

14
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.964
funds and treasury and come out on a schedule.

15
00:00:44.964 --> 00:00:46.654
Every unlock adds sellers.

16
00:00:47.654 --> 00:00:52.388
Even the largest coins have fallen by 80 and even 96 percent from their peak and

17
00:00:52.388 --> 00:00:54.163
taken years to get back to it.

18
00:00:54.163 --> 00:00:55.820
And these are the survivors:

19
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.488
thousands of coins from past cycles have disappeared entirely.

20
00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:04.459
That is why the chart of crypto as a whole looks better than the result of a typical

21
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:04.814
buyer.

22
00:01:05.814 --> 00:01:08.587
An option is a right, but not an obligation,

23
00:01:08.587 --> 00:01:12.306
to buy or sell a coin at an agreed price on a certain date.

24
00:01:12.306 --> 00:01:15.394
For the right, the buyer pays a premium up front.

25
00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:20.310
A call profits from a rise; a put profits from a fall and resembles insurance.

26
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.020
The buyer's loss is limited to the premium.

27
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:28.062
The seller's profit is also limited to the premium, while the loss can be large.

28
00:01:29.062 --> 00:01:32.187
The main thing in an option's price is volatility:

29
00:01:32.187 --> 00:01:36.436
the more a coin swings, the likelier the right turns out profitable,

30
00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:37.748
and the dearer it is.

31
00:01:37.748 --> 00:01:42.623
In the lesson the Black–Scholes model converts expected swings into a premium.

32
00:01:42.623 --> 00:01:45.310
Try changing the strike price and the term.

33
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.212
A dated future is a contract to buy or sell a coin on a certain date at a price

34
00:01:51.212 --> 00:01:52.019
agreed today.

35
00:01:52.019 --> 00:01:55.928
The gap between the future's price and the coin's is the basis.

36
00:01:55.928 --> 00:02:00.396
When many want to buy with leverage, the future is dearer than the coin.

37
00:02:00.396 --> 00:02:03.374
And by the expiry date the basis must disappear.

38
00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:05.199
Hence a trade:

39
00:02:05.199 --> 00:02:08.144
buy the coin and sell the future at the same time.

40
00:02:08.144 --> 00:02:10.913
What you lose on one leg you gain on the other,

41
00:02:10.913 --> 00:02:13.328
and what remains is the basis minus fees.

42
00:02:13.328 --> 00:02:17.570
It resembles a deposit, with the risk of the exchange holding both legs,

43
00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:22.518
and the risk of a sharp rise, when you are asked to add collateral on the short leg.

44
00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:28.453
Tokenized assets promise to bring bonds, gold and real estate onto the blockchain.

45
00:02:28.453 --> 00:02:33.449
But the blockchain only stores a record of who has how many tokens and does not see

46
00:02:33.449 --> 00:02:34.472
the asset itself.

47
00:02:34.472 --> 00:02:36.939
So you have to check the chain of people:

48
00:02:36.939 --> 00:02:39.287
who the issuer is, who holds the asset,

49
00:02:39.287 --> 00:02:42.176
whether an ordinary holder can redeem the token,

50
00:02:42.176 --> 00:02:44.884
and what happens if the issuer goes bankrupt.

51
00:02:44.884 --> 00:02:46.870
There are no yield miracles here.

52
00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:48.857
The main points.

53
00:02:48.857 --> 00:02:50.956
An option is a right with a price:

54
00:02:50.956 --> 00:02:55.584
the buyer's loss is limited to the premium, the seller's profit is limited.

55
00:02:55.584 --> 00:02:57.683
With a coin the tokenomics matter:

56
00:02:57.683 --> 00:03:00.275
how much is still to come and who owns it.

57
00:03:00.275 --> 00:03:05.397
And the largest coins have fallen almost to zero from their peak and taken years to

58
00:03:05.397 --> 00:03:06.014
come back.
